# The kernels reproduce R's arithmetic, and R rounds the product `x * w` to
# double before accumulating it, because `sum(x * w)` evaluates the product
# into a double vector first. Compilers default to -ffp-contract=fast, which
# may contract a multiply followed by an add into a fused multiply-add even
# across the intervening assignment, skipping that rounding. On x86-64 under
# -O2 there is no FMA instruction to contract into, so nothing changed; on
# arm64, where FMA is baseline, the weighted means drifted in the last bits and
# the engine stopped matching the R implementation it exists to reproduce.
#
# Confirmed by reproducing the arm64 condition on x86-64: with LDOUBLE forced
# to double, -ffp-contract=fast and -ffp-contract=off disagree on 293 of 400
# random weighted means, by up to 1.1e-16. With the real 80-bit long double
# they agree exactly even under -mfma, because a double multiply followed by a
# long double add is not a fusable pattern - which is why x86-64 never showed
# this and arm64, where long double IS double, did.
#
# Turning contraction off costs nothing measurable here and is what keeps the
# bit-for-bit claim true on every platform.
PKG_CXXFLAGS = -ffp-contract=off
